%% bibliography.bib -- LeanRustLisp, JOT revision (paper/jot_r1)
%%
%% Merged from the submitted paper's bibliography (paper/jcl_v2/bibliography.bib; all keys
%% cited by paper/jcl_v2/main.tex kept) and the two literature reports of the revision
%% (related_linear_ownership, related_rust_macros_inplace). One entry per work: four
%% conference/journal pairs with identical titles were reduced to one entry each (the other
%% version is named in a comment above the kept entry).
%%
%% AUDIT NOTE (CLAUDE.md no-fabrication rule). Every entry was checked on 2026-10-05; the
%% line "%% verified:" above each entry says how. Methods:
%%   - DOI entries: Crossref REST API (curl https://api.crossref.org/works/<DOI>), or the
%%     DataCite API (https://api.datacite.org/dois/<DOI>) for 10.48550/* and 10.4230/* DOIs;
%%     title, author family names, year, venue, volume, issue and pages compared field by
%%     field by a script. LNCS/LNBIP volumes, editors and book subtitles from the Crossref
%%     unixsd XML (.../works/<DOI>/transform/application/vnd.crossref.unixsd+xml) and the
%%     Crossref record of the parent book.
%%   - PACMPL article numbers ("N:1--N:M") only where the paper PDF's ACM Reference Format
%%     block or running footer was read; otherwise Crossref's page range is used.
%%   - Entries without DOI: the cited URL itself, the GitHub API, OpenLibrary, author pages.
%% A field that could not be verified is omitted (never guessed); deviations from the
%% Crossref record are explained in a comment above the entry.
%% Checked with bibtex and the JOT class's style (jot.cls loads apacite and natbib and sets
%% \bibliographystyle{apacite}): with \nocite{*}, bibtex reports no errors and no warnings.
%%
%% Sections follow the related-work lines of CHANGE_LIST.md section 4:
%%   L1 linear and dependent type theories; L2 refinement types and verification for Rust;
%%   L3 dependent type systems through hygienic macros (and macro systems);
%%   L4 ownership types in the object-oriented tradition; L5 in-place update;
%%   then Rust semantics, type-theory foundations, dependently typed languages, other.

